WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.195
Lesson 2.2.

00:00:02.195 --> 00:00:05.885
AMM mathematics: x times y equals k.

00:00:05.885 --> 00:00:08.316
By the end, you'll calculate what a swap

00:00:08.316 --> 00:00:11.280
actually pays out in a constant-product pool, and

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:13.830
explain why bigger trades get worse prices.

00:00:15.130 --> 00:00:16.420
Here's the plan.

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:19.050
The one rule a constant-product pool actually

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:21.465
keeps, and what it means for pricing.

00:00:21.465 --> 00:00:23.710
How price impact falls directly out of

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:26.140
that rule, not from any separate fee.

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:28.090
Who rebalances the pool after your

00:00:28.090 --> 00:00:30.285
trade, and why that matters later.

00:00:30.285 --> 00:00:32.517
And a full worked example, with real

00:00:32.517 --> 00:00:34.810
numbers, at two different trade sizes.

00:00:36.110 --> 00:00:36.965
The one rule.

00:00:38.265 --> 00:00:40.237
Here's the entire mechanism, in

00:00:40.237 --> 00:00:42.570
one sentence, before any numbers.

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:46.455
A constant-product pool holds two tokens, and keeps one

00:00:46.455 --> 00:00:49.855
rule: the reserves of token A, multiplied by the

00:00:49.855 --> 00:00:53.555
reserves of token B, equals a constant, called k.

00:00:53.555 --> 00:00:56.810
No order book, no matching, just this one product,

00:00:56.810 --> 00:01:00.320
held constant, ignoring fees, after every single trade.

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:04.565
Here's what “pool price” means, concretely.

00:01:04.565 --> 00:01:07.130
It's simply the ratio of the two reserves.

00:01:07.130 --> 00:01:11.146
A hundred E.T.H. and three hundred thousand U.S.D.C.

00:01:11.146 --> 00:01:13.949
in the same pool means one E.T.H. is

00:01:13.949 --> 00:01:18.344
priced at roughly three thousand U.S.D.C., because that's

00:01:18.344 --> 00:01:21.750
the ratio those two reserve numbers make.

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:24.090
Change either reserve, and that ratio,

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:25.710
the price, changes with it.

00:01:27.010 --> 00:01:30.418
Here's what actually happens, mechanically, the instant

00:01:30.418 --> 00:01:32.605
you swap E.T.H. into that pool.

00:01:32.605 --> 00:01:35.490
You add E.T.H. to the pool's reserves.

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:38.300
To keep x times y equal to that same

00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:42.933
constant k, the pool must remove U.S.D.C., automatically, by

00:01:42.933 --> 00:01:46.195
the formula alone, not by any decision.

00:01:46.195 --> 00:01:48.899
And what you receive is exactly however

00:01:48.899 --> 00:01:51.400
much U.S.D.C. the pool had to remove

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:53.360
to keep the product constant.

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:56.520
Worth seeing the same rule run in

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:59.665
the other direction, since it's perfectly symmetric.

00:01:59.665 --> 00:02:03.314
Swap U.S.D.C. into the pool instead, and E.T.H. is

00:02:03.314 --> 00:02:06.605
what has to leave, by the exact same formula,

00:02:06.605 --> 00:02:10.125
no exception for which token moves which way.

00:02:10.125 --> 00:02:13.160
Add U.S.D.C. to the pool's reserves.

00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:15.860
To keep x times y equal to k, the

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:19.672
pool must remove E.T.H. And what you receive is

00:02:19.672 --> 00:02:23.245
exactly however much E.T.H. it had to remove.

00:02:24.545 --> 00:02:25.635
Quick check.

00:02:25.635 --> 00:02:29.139
A pool holds fifty E.T.H. and one hundred fifty

00:02:29.139 --> 00:02:33.080
thousand U.S.D.C. What's k, and what's the spot price?

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:40.252
The answer: k is seven million five hundred

00:02:40.252 --> 00:02:43.930
thousand, from fifty times one hundred fifty thousand.

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:46.877
Spot price is three thousand USDC per ETH,

00:02:46.877 --> 00:02:49.275
from the ratio of the two reserves.

00:02:50.575 --> 00:02:52.180
Where price impact comes from.

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:57.325
Here's the actual shape underneath every constant-product trade.

00:02:57.325 --> 00:03:00.018
Plot the two reserves against each other,

00:03:00.018 --> 00:03:02.262
and every point that keeps x times

00:03:02.262 --> 00:03:04.550
y equal to k traces this curve.

00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:07.025
Your trade is a move along it: add to

00:03:07.025 --> 00:03:10.255
one axis, the other axis falls to compensate.

00:03:10.255 --> 00:03:12.880
And because the curve bends, moving further

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:15.506
along it costs progressively more per unit,

00:03:15.506 --> 00:03:17.415
not a constant amount each time.

00:03:18.715 --> 00:03:21.268
This is the exact reason price impact

00:03:21.268 --> 00:03:23.650
isn't a fee anyone charges you.

00:03:23.650 --> 00:03:27.080
It's a direct, mechanical consequence of the curve's shape.

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:28.583
The first unit of your trade

00:03:28.583 --> 00:03:30.335
moves the reserves a little.

00:03:30.335 --> 00:03:32.553
The last unit of the same trade moves

00:03:32.553 --> 00:03:35.704
them from an already-shifted starting point, which is

00:03:35.704 --> 00:03:38.214
why it's priced worse than the first unit,

00:03:38.214 --> 00:03:40.140
within the very same transaction.

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:44.967
Here's how to actually calculate price impact yourself,

00:03:44.967 --> 00:03:47.424
on any pool, using the exact same four

00:03:47.424 --> 00:03:49.865
moves as the worked example ahead.

00:03:49.865 --> 00:03:52.110
Read the pool's two current reserves.

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:53.201
Add your trade size to

00:03:53.201 --> 00:03:54.925
the reserve you're putting in.

00:03:54.925 --> 00:03:57.664
Divide k, the original product, by that new

00:03:57.664 --> 00:04:00.640
reserve, to get the other side's new value.

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:03.345
And compare what you'd receive against the pool's

00:04:03.345 --> 00:04:05.888
current spot price, to see the real percentage.

00:04:07.188 --> 00:04:08.278
Quick check.

00:04:08.278 --> 00:04:10.371
Why does a 10 ETH swap get

00:04:10.371 --> 00:04:12.775
a worse average price than a 1

00:04:12.775 --> 00:04:15.333
ETH swap, in the exact same pool?

00:04:19.753 --> 00:04:22.155
The answer: each unit you add moves the

00:04:22.155 --> 00:04:25.036
reserve ratio further along the curve, so later

00:04:25.036 --> 00:04:28.038
units within the same trade are priced worse.

00:04:28.038 --> 00:04:31.118
Price impact grows with size relative to reserves.

00:04:32.418 --> 00:04:33.458
Arbitrageurs.

00:04:34.758 --> 00:04:37.862
Here's what happens right after your trade shifts the

00:04:37.862 --> 00:04:40.921
pool's ratio away from the wider market's price.

00:04:40.921 --> 00:04:43.143
An arbitrageur notices the pool is

00:04:43.143 --> 00:04:46.046
now priced differently from other venues.

00:04:46.046 --> 00:04:47.813
They trade against the pool, in

00:04:47.813 --> 00:04:50.101
whichever direction closes that gap.

00:04:50.101 --> 00:04:52.413
That trade pulls the pool's ratio back

00:04:52.413 --> 00:04:54.491
in line with the wider market.

00:04:54.491 --> 00:04:56.842
And it also changes exactly what the

00:04:56.842 --> 00:04:59.764
pool's liquidity providers now hold, which is

00:04:59.764 --> 00:05:02.496
where impermanent loss actually comes from.

00:05:03.796 --> 00:05:05.810
Worth being precise about why this

00:05:05.810 --> 00:05:07.951
matters beyond just this lesson.

00:05:07.951 --> 00:05:10.117
Arbitrage isn't a flaw in the system;

00:05:10.117 --> 00:05:12.284
it's what keeps a pool's price honest

00:05:12.284 --> 00:05:14.351
against the rest of the market.

00:05:14.351 --> 00:05:17.030
But every time it happens, the pool's liquidity

00:05:17.030 --> 00:05:19.485
providers end up holding a different mix of

00:05:19.485 --> 00:05:21.906
the two tokens than they started with.

00:05:21.906 --> 00:05:24.730
Lesson two point four picks this up directly, and

00:05:24.730 --> 00:05:27.271
shows exactly how to measure what that costs.

00:05:28.571 --> 00:05:29.576
Worked example.

00:05:30.876 --> 00:05:33.801
Here's the pool this entire example runs on.

00:05:33.801 --> 00:05:38.092
100 ETH, 300,000 USDC. Multiply those

00:05:38.092 --> 00:05:41.666
reserves and k is 30,000,000.

00:05:41.666 --> 00:05:44.668
Divide them and spot price is 3,000

00:05:44.668 --> 00:05:48.922
USDC/ETH. Every number that follows falls directly

00:05:48.922 --> 00:05:51.841
out of these two starting reserves.

00:05:53.141 --> 00:05:55.284
Now swap in ten E.T.H., fees

00:05:55.284 --> 00:05:58.761
ignored, and follow the arithmetic exactly.

00:05:58.761 --> 00:06:01.312
New E.T.H. reserve: one hundred plus

00:06:01.312 --> 00:06:03.266
ten, is one hundred ten.

00:06:03.266 --> 00:06:06.681
New U.S.D.C. reserve: thirty million divided by

00:06:06.681 --> 00:06:09.526
one hundred ten, is roughly two hundred

00:06:09.526 --> 00:06:13.241
seventy-two thousand seven hundred twenty-seven.

00:06:13.241 --> 00:06:16.834
You receive the difference: three hundred thousand minus

00:06:16.834 --> 00:06:21.246
that, roughly twenty-seven thousand two hundred seventy-three U.S.D.C.

00:06:22.546 --> 00:06:24.974
Put that effective price next to spot, and

00:06:24.974 --> 00:06:27.251
here's the real cost of this trade.

00:06:27.251 --> 00:06:31.715
Twenty-seven hundred twenty-seven U.S.D.C. per E.T.H., against

00:06:31.715 --> 00:06:34.211
a spot price of three thousand.

00:06:34.211 --> 00:06:36.907
That's roughly nine point one percent worse

00:06:36.907 --> 00:06:39.481
than spot, entirely from this one trade's

00:06:39.481 --> 00:06:41.871
own size against this one pool's depth.

00:06:43.171 --> 00:06:44.902
Now the same pool, the same

00:06:44.902 --> 00:06:47.366
formula, a trade ten times smaller.

00:06:47.366 --> 00:06:50.732
One E.T.H. in: new reserve one hundred one, new

00:06:50.732 --> 00:06:55.640
U.S.D.C. reserve roughly two hundred ninety-seven thousand thirty, so

00:06:55.640 --> 00:06:59.707
you receive about two thousand nine hundred seventy, only

00:06:59.707 --> 00:07:02.461
around one percent worse than spot.

00:07:02.461 --> 00:07:05.451
Ten times the size cost roughly nine times

00:07:05.451 --> 00:07:08.301
more in price impact, not ten times, but

00:07:08.301 --> 00:07:11.221
close, and worth internalising either way.

00:07:12.521 --> 00:07:14.943
Push it further still, same pool, same

00:07:14.943 --> 00:07:17.728
formula, to see how sharply this curves.

00:07:17.728 --> 00:07:21.379
Swap in 50 ETH, half the pool's original

00:07:21.379 --> 00:07:25.743
depth: new reserve 150, new USDC reserve exactly

00:07:25.743 --> 00:07:29.662
200,000, so you receive 100,000 USDC, at an

00:07:29.662 --> 00:07:34.026
effective price of 2,000 per ETH. That's roughly

00:07:34.026 --> 00:07:37.499
33% worse than spot, from a trade only

00:07:37.499 --> 00:07:40.883
5 times larger than the first example.

00:07:42.183 --> 00:07:45.011
Plot all three sizes together, and the shape of

00:07:45.011 --> 00:07:48.670
that earlier curve becomes obvious in the actual numbers.

00:07:48.670 --> 00:07:51.575
One E.T.H., about one percent worse.

00:07:51.575 --> 00:07:54.558
Ten E.T.H., about nine percent worse.

00:07:54.558 --> 00:07:57.953
Fifty E.T.H., about thirty-three percent worse.

00:07:57.953 --> 00:07:59.538
It isn't a straight line.

00:07:59.538 --> 00:08:02.987
Each step up in size costs a disproportionately bigger

00:08:02.987 --> 00:08:06.625
bite, exactly because you're moving further along a curve

00:08:06.625 --> 00:08:09.008
that bends, not a flat, constant rate.

00:08:10.308 --> 00:08:13.114
So here's the one sentence this entire worked

00:08:13.114 --> 00:08:17.013
example proves, worth remembering over any specific number.

00:08:17.013 --> 00:08:20.322
Size relative to pool depth is what actually matters,

00:08:20.322 --> 00:08:23.318
not the size of your trade in dollars alone.

00:08:23.318 --> 00:08:26.204
The same ten E.T.H. against a much deeper

00:08:26.204 --> 00:08:28.953
pool would barely move the price at all.

00:08:30.253 --> 00:08:31.343
Quick check.

00:08:31.343 --> 00:08:33.860
Who actually moves the pool's price back in

00:08:33.860 --> 00:08:36.262
line with the wider market after a trade,

00:08:36.262 --> 00:08:38.893
and why does it matter to liquidity providers?

00:08:43.313 --> 00:08:45.348
The answer: arbitrageurs.

00:08:45.348 --> 00:08:47.895
Their trades rebalance the pool, which is

00:08:47.895 --> 00:08:50.866
exactly where impermanent loss comes from, since

00:08:50.866 --> 00:08:52.988
it changes what LPs end up holding.

00:08:54.287 --> 00:08:56.632
Here's your checklist, before any swap.

00:08:56.632 --> 00:08:59.349
Check the price impact the interface shows; if

00:08:59.349 --> 00:09:01.778
it isn't shown, work it out yourself, the

00:09:01.778 --> 00:09:03.812
same way this lesson just did.

00:09:03.812 --> 00:09:05.912
Compare with an aggregator quote.

00:09:05.912 --> 00:09:09.079
Set your slippage tolerance on purpose: too tight

00:09:09.079 --> 00:09:11.676
and the swap simply fails; too loose and

00:09:11.676 --> 00:09:13.830
you risk a bad fill or a sandwich

00:09:13.830 --> 00:09:17.107
attack, covered fully in Lesson two point five.

00:09:17.107 --> 00:09:19.882
Split large trades, or use deeper pools.

00:09:19.882 --> 00:09:22.656
And run the full before-signing checklist:

00:09:22.656 --> 00:09:25.237
chain, contract, token, amount, spender.

00:09:26.537 --> 00:09:27.782
Let's recap.

00:09:27.782 --> 00:09:30.674
One rule: reserves times reserves equals

00:09:30.674 --> 00:09:33.442
k, held constant after every trade.

00:09:33.442 --> 00:09:35.611
Price impact isn't a fee; it's the

00:09:35.611 --> 00:09:38.462
direct, mechanical shape of that curve, worse

00:09:38.462 --> 00:09:41.117
for later units within the same trade.

00:09:41.117 --> 00:09:44.475
Arbitrageurs rebalance the pool back to the wider market,

00:09:44.475 --> 00:09:47.727
which is exactly where impermanent loss comes from.

00:09:47.727 --> 00:09:50.806
And size relative to pool depth is what actually

00:09:50.806 --> 00:09:53.697
decides your price, not the dollar size alone.

00:09:54.997 --> 00:09:57.455
Work out the price impact yourself, on any

00:09:57.455 --> 00:10:00.842
pool, before you trust the interface's number blindly.

00:10:00.842 --> 00:10:04.017
Next up, Lesson 2.3: Providing liquidity.
